;;; 78-svg-path.slat — Motoi Scheme Reference · Chapter 78 · library svg-path
;;;
;;; 3 verbs in this library. Read chapter 00-intro.slat first.
;;;
;;; ─── THE ROW RULES (summary — full doctrine in 00-intro.slat) ───
;;;
;;;   Row 1 (HOW · Composition) — the verb written well; 1/3/5 module ladder
;;;   Row 2 (WHEN · Audit)      — operator's problem + this-verb-not-a-sibling
;;;   Row 3 (WHERE ELSE · Dim)  — same shape in 3 substrates (verb/metaphor/math)
;;;   Row 4 (WHY · Proof)       — claim + runnable test + ∀-invariant
;;;   Row 5 (WITH WHAT ELSE)    — chained verbs → named emergent pattern
;;;
;;; Every row carries the three-discipline lens: :eng · :math · :cs.
;;; Every code block RUNS through ./bin/motoi. Made by Alfred Borden.
;;;
;;; This library is the bridge from the whole open-icon world (Feather,
;;; Lucide, Bootstrap, Material glyphs) into Motoi pixels. An SVG path `d`
;;; string is a tiny command language — M moveto, L lineto, H/V axis-lines,
;;; C cubic Bézier, Q quadratic Bézier, Z close — in uppercase (absolute)
;;; or lowercase (relative). This module reads that subset, flattens the
;;; Béziers into line segments, and hands back a pixel-native POINT LIST you
;;; can stroke or fill. It is how Motoi acquires a real vocabulary of named
;;; things (a heart, a star, an arrow) without hand-inventing each one.
;;;

(dialect :applies ("motoi") :note "Library svg-path — 3 verbs.")

(chapter :number "78" :library "svg-path" :verb-count 3 :training-eligible #t)

;; ═══ 3 verbs in library svg-path ═══

(verb
  :name "svg-path->points" :std "Motoi"
  :library "svg-path"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(svg-path->points d [scale] [ox] [oy] [steps]) -> list-of-(x y)"
  :summary "Parse an SVG path string into a flattened list of (x y) points, scaling and offsetting them onto the canvas coordinate space."
  :explanation "This is the pure reader at the heart of the library: it takes a path `d` string — the same mini-language every icon set speaks — and folds it into a single list of (x y) points, one polyline. M and L place and connect points; H and V move along one axis; C and Q are Béziers that get SAMPLED at `steps` points each (default 8) so a curve becomes a chain of short line segments; Z appends the subpath's start again to close the loop. Uppercase letters are absolute, lowercase are relative to the current point. `scale` multiplies every coordinate, and (ox, oy) shift the whole shape — that is how you take a glyph drawn in a 0..24 viewBox and land it, sized, anywhere on the 320×240 screen. Because it PAINTS NOTHING and just returns data, you can measure, transform, or replicate the point list before ever touching the framebuffer — no set-mode required."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(length (svg-path->points \"M12 2 L2 22 L22 22 Z\" 4 40 40))" :note "A triangle glyph (three corners plus a Z that returns to the start) scaled ×4 and shifted to (40,40) parses to four points.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(map (lambda (d) (length (svg-path->points d 2 0 0)))\n     (list \"M12 2 L22 12 L12 22 L2 12 Z\"\n           \"M10 4 H14 V10 H20 V14 H14 V20 H10 V14 H4 V10 H10 Z\"))" :note "Read a whole batch of glyph strings — a diamond and a plus-sign built from H/V axis-lines — and report how many points each flattens to.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define arrow \"M4 12 H20 M14 6 L20 12 L14 18\")\n(define pts (svg-path->points arrow 3 0 0 8))\n(define (bbox ps)\n  (let ((xs (map car ps)) (ys (map cadr ps)))\n    (list 'x0 (fold min (car xs) xs) 'y0 (fold min (car ys) ys)\n          'x1 (fold max (car xs) xs) 'y1 (fold max (car ys) ys))))\n(list 'points (length pts) 'extent (bbox pts))" :note "Parse a two-subpath arrow (a shaft plus a head), then fold the point list down to its bounding box — geometry you can inspect before drawing.")
  )
  :caveats ("Only M/L/H/V/C/Q/Z are understood, in both absolute (uppercase) and relative (lowercase) forms. S, T, and A are NOT parsed: the reader skips a single number to stay out of an infinite loop, so a smooth-shorthand curve (S/T) or an elliptical arc (A) contributes NO geometry — the shape degrades to whatever the supported commands drew. Verify with a supported subset if precision matters." "Multiple subpaths (more than one M) are concatenated into ONE point list, not kept separate. A Z closes back to the most recent subpath start, so a two-hole glyph becomes a single connected polyline — fine for stroking, but even-odd fill of it can surprise you." "Curves are flattened, not exact: `steps` line segments approximate each Bézier, so a coarse `steps` gives a visibly faceted arc. Higher `steps` is smoother but returns more points.")
  :drawbacks ("You get a flat point list, not a structured path — the command boundaries, subpath breaks, and which points came from a curve are all lost once flattening happens. If you need per-command control, parse the `d` string yourself." "There is no winding-rule choice here: the list is just points. The fill verb applies even-odd; this reader cannot hand you a nonzero-winding polygon." "Very long `d` strings with many high-`steps` curves produce large lists; the cost is O(commands + steps·curves) in both time and memory.")
  :usecases ("Importing an icon (Feather/Lucide/Bootstrap/Material) as points you can stroke or fill on the canvas." "Measuring a glyph — its bounding box, point count, or centroid — before placing it, so layout is data-driven." "Pre-transforming a shape (scale, mirror, replicate) in list-space before any painting happens." "Turning a designed logo path into a reusable point list you stamp many times in a scene.")
  :related ("draw-svg-path" "fill-svg-path" "poly" "fill-poly")
  :learn (:concept "An SVG path is a command stream; parsing it means folding tokens into a geometry (a point list), with Béziers sampled into line segments." :prerequisites ("iota" "fold" "map") :progression "Once you can read a path to points, stroke it with draw-svg-path or fill it with fill-svg-path; then combine the two to build layered icons.")
  :row-2-audit (

    (:problem "A UI builder has pulled a chevron glyph out of an icon set as the path \"M8 4 L16 12 L8 20\" and wants to place it in a menu row. Before drawing, they need to know how wide it will be at scale 3 so the row spacing is right — a question about geometry, not pixels, and they do not want to paint just to measure."
     :program "(let* ((d \"M8 4 L16 12 L8 20\")\n       (pts (svg-path->points d 3 0 0))\n       (xs (map car pts)))\n  (list 'width (- (fold max (car xs) xs) (fold min (car xs) xs))))"
     :eng-program "(define (glyph-extent d scale)\n  (let* ((pts (svg-path->points d scale 0 0))\n         (xs (map car pts)) (ys (map cadr pts)))\n    (list 'w (- (fold max (car xs) xs) (fold min (car xs) xs))\n          'h (- (fold max (car ys) ys) (fold min (car ys) ys))\n          'n (length pts))))\n(glyph-extent \"M8 4 L16 12 L8 20\" 3)"
     :math-program "(define (bbox-invariant d)\n  ;; width at scale 2 must be exactly twice the width at scale 1.\n  (define (w s) (let* ((xs (map car (svg-path->points d s 0 0))))\n                  (- (fold max (car xs) xs) (fold min (car xs) xs))))\n  (list 'w1 (w 1) 'w2 (w 2) 'doubles? (= (* 2 (w 1)) (w 2))))\n(bbox-invariant \"M8 4 L16 12 L8 20\")"
     :cs-program "(define (points-or-empty? d scale)\n  (let ((pts (svg-path->points d scale 0 0)))\n    (and (list? pts) (or (null? pts)\n                         (and (list? (car pts)) (= (length (car pts)) 2))))))\n(points-or-empty? \"M8 4 L16 12 L8 20\" 3)"
     :explanation "eng: return width, height, and point count in one row for layout. math: the bounding box scales linearly with `scale`, so width×2 is the invariant to check. cs: type-guard — the result is always a list of two-element points (possibly empty)."
     :meta "draw-svg-path and fill-svg-path would ANSWER the width question only by painting, which requires set-mode and destroys the pure measurement the builder wants. poly consumes a point list but cannot read a `d` string. svg-path->points is the parse-only primitive; picking either painter forces a framebuffer round trip just to learn a number.")
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'measure before you paint' — a layout smoke test reads the point list and asserts the bounding box fits the slot; if a glyph overflows, the caller catches it in list-space, not on screen after the fact." :math "The map d → points is affine in (scale, ox, oy): every returned coordinate is ox + scale·x_raw, so the bounding box is an affine image of the raw box." :cs "A tokenizer-then-interpreter: a regex splits the string into command letters and numbers, a state machine folds them into the point list; cost is O(commands + steps·curves)."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Read a linear command stream and fold it into a geometric structure — a cursor moves and records where it went."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "svg-path" :code "(svg-path->points \"M12 2 L2 22 L22 22 Z\" 4 0 0)")
       (:domain "turtle"   :code "(define (turtle cmds)\n  (let loop ((cs cmds) (x 0) (y 0) (acc (list (list 0 0))))\n    (if (null? cs) (reverse acc)\n        (let* ((c (car cs)) (dx (cadr c)) (dy (caddr c))\n               (nx (+ x dx)) (ny (+ y dy)))\n          (loop (cdr cs) nx ny (cons (list nx ny) acc))))))\n(turtle '((forward 10 0) (forward 0 10) (forward -10 0)))")
       (:domain "polynomial-sampling" :code "(define (cubic-at p0 p1 p2 p3 t)\n  (let ((u (- 1 t)))\n    (+ (* u u u p0) (* 3 u u t p1) (* 3 u t t p2) (* t t t p3))))\n(map (lambda (i) (cubic-at 0 10 30 40 (/ i 4.0))) (iota 5))"))
     :explanation "Rung 1: an SVG path is a stream of move/line/curve commands folded into a list of points a cursor visited. Rung 2: a LOGO turtle reads forward/turn commands and records the trail its pen left. Rung 3: a cubic Bézier IS a degree-3 polynomial in t, and flattening it is just sampling that polynomial at t = i/steps. All three share the same shape — a command stream drives a moving state, and the states it passes through ARE the geometry."
     :meta "The transfer works because 'fold a token stream into a structure' is substrate-independent. A plotter reading G-code, a turtle reading LOGO, a parser reading a path — each is a cursor that consumes instructions and leaves a geometric trail. The Bézier rung shows the math underneath: flattening a curve is numerically sampling a polynomial. Grounds in book-of-math ch-advanced-10 (numerical-methods) as the sample-a-parametric-curve shape.")
     (:eng "Every command-stream reader shares one failure mode — an unrecognized instruction. The SRE fix here is a conservative skip (consume one number, keep going) so a bad token degrades the shape instead of hanging the parser." :math "A parametric curve γ(t) on [0,1] is discretized at t_i = i/steps; the polyline through {γ(t_i)} converges to γ as steps grows, with error bounded by the curve's curvature." :cs "Lexer + pushdown-free state machine: tokens in, a cursor (cx, cy) and subpath-start (sx, sy) as state, points out — the classic streaming-parser fold."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "Scaling is linear on coordinates: every point of (svg-path->points d 2 0 0) is exactly twice the corresponding point of (svg-path->points d 1 0 0)."
     :program "(define d \"M12 2 L2 22 L22 22 Z\")\n(define a (svg-path->points d 1 0 0))\n(define b (svg-path->points d 2 0 0))\n(display (equal? (map (lambda (p) (list (* 2 (car p)) (* 2 (cadr p)))) a) b))\n(newline)\n(define e (svg-path->points d 3 0 0))\n(display (equal? (map (lambda (p) (list (* 3 (car p)) (* 3 (cadr p)))) a) e))"
     :eng-program "(define (scale-audit d scales)\n  (let ((base (svg-path->points d 1 0 0)))\n    (map (lambda (s)\n           (let ((scaled (svg-path->points d s 0 0)))\n             (list 's s 'linear?\n                   (equal? (map (lambda (p) (list (* s (car p)) (* s (cadr p)))) base)\n                           scaled))))\n         scales)))\n(scale-audit \"M12 2 L2 22 L22 22 Z\" '(1 2 3 5))"
     :math-program "(define (closed? d s ox oy)\n  ;; A path ending in Z returns to its start: first point equals last point.\n  (let ((p (svg-path->points d s ox oy)))\n    (equal? (car p) (car (reverse p)))))\n(list 'triangle (closed? \"M12 2 L2 22 L22 22 Z\" 3 10 10)\n      'diamond  (closed? \"M12 2 L22 12 L12 22 L2 12 Z\" 4 0 0))"
     :cs-program "(define (steps-monotone? d lo hi)\n  ;; A curve sampled with more steps yields at least as many points.\n  (>= (length (svg-path->points d 1 0 0 hi))\n      (length (svg-path->points d 1 0 0 lo))))\n(steps-monotone? \"M0 0 Q 5 10 10 0\" 3 12)"
     :invariant "∀ d scale. points(d, k·scale, 0, 0) = k · points(d, scale, 0, 0)  (coordinates are linear in scale; and a Z-closed path has first point = last point; and steps↑ ⇒ point-count↑ or =)"
     :meta "The CS pattern is that the parser is a HOMOMORPHISM from scale-multiplication to coordinate-multiplication — reading then scaling equals scaling then reading, because scale is applied uniformly at the end. Falsified if doubling `scale` did not double every coordinate — that would mean the reader is doing something nonlinear (rounding early, clamping to the canvas), and every layout computed in list-space would be wrong. book-of-math ch-advanced-04 (groups-symmetry) grounds the linear-map / homomorphism shape; book-of-code ch 05 grounds the two-paths-same-answer check."
     )
    (:eng "The engineering consequence is that a designer can compute layout at scale 1 and multiply — no need to re-parse per size; the width of a glyph at scale 5 is five times its width at scale 1, provable in one line." :math "The point map is γ ↦ ox·𝟙 + scale·γ; fixing (ox,oy)=0 makes it the linear map γ ↦ scale·γ, which commutes with scalar multiplication." :cs "Linearity / homomorphism invariant; the same shape as len(map f xs) = len xs — a structural property preserved by a uniform transform."))
  :row-5-emergence (

    (:base "svg-path->points reads one glyph string into one flat point list."
     :composition "(define diamond \"M12 2 L22 12 L12 22 L2 12 Z\")\n(define (stamp-row d n dx y scale)\n  (map (lambda (i)\n         (map (lambda (p) (list (+ (* i dx) (car p)) (+ y (cadr p))))\n              (svg-path->points d scale 0 0)))\n       (iota n)))\n(length (stamp-row diamond 5 40 30 2))"
     :eng-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define arrow \"M4 12 H20 M14 6 L20 12 L14 18\")\n(for-each (lambda (i) (draw-svg-path arrow (+ 20 (* i 40)) 100 3 7))\n          (iota 6))"
     :math-composition "(define star \"M12 2 L15 9 L22 9 L16 14 L18 22 L12 17 L6 22 L8 14 L2 9 L9 9 Z\")\n(define pts (svg-path->points star 2 0 0))\n(define (centroid ps)\n  (let ((n (length ps)))\n    (list (/ (apply + (map car ps)) n)\n          (/ (apply + (map cadr ps)) n))))\n(list 'points (length pts) 'centroid (centroid pts))"
     :cs-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define glyphs (list \"M12 2 L2 22 L22 22 Z\" \"M12 2 L22 12 L12 22 L2 12 Z\"))\n(for-each (lambda (i)\n            (fill-svg-path (list-ref glyphs (modulo i 2))\n                           (+ 40 (* i 50)) 60 2 (+ 2 i)))\n          (iota 4))"
     :emergent "An icon set: one imported glyph is replicated across a row of offsets (a toolbar of arrows), or a whole alphabet of shapes is read and laid out in a strip. Together they are a retained-geometry / vector-graphics-import pipeline — read once into data, then place many times — structurally the same as a sprite atlas or a font's glyph table."
     :meta "This is the vector-graphics-import / retained-geometry family — parse a resource once into a value, then transform and replicate that value cheaply. Familiar analogs are a sprite sheet indexed by frame, a font renderer laying out glyph outlines, and a scene graph instancing one mesh at many transforms. The composition works because svg-path->points is PURE (Row 2) and LINEAR in placement (Row 4), so a glyph read once can be shifted and scaled in list-space without re-parsing. Grounds in book-of-code ch 14 (assembly-then-higher) as the parse-once-instance-many shape."
     )
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'parse once, stamp many' — reading the glyph inside the stamp loop re-tokenizes the string every time, so hoist the svg-path->points call out of the loop; the SRE probe is 'how many times did the parser run per frame?'" :math "Placement is an affine action of the translation group on the point set; replicating a glyph at n offsets is the orbit of the base geometry under {translate by iΔ}." :cs "Parse-once-instance-many; the shape of every asset pipeline where a decoded resource is cached and drawn at many transforms."))
)

(verb
  :name "draw-svg-path" :std "Motoi"
  :library "svg-path"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(draw-svg-path d ox oy scale [color]) -> undefined"
  :summary "Stroke an SVG path onto the canvas as a connected outline of line segments; requires (set-mode 'sd)."
  :explanation "Where svg-path->points hands you data, this verb paints: it parses the `d` string, places it at (ox, oy) at the given `scale`, and draws the resulting polyline as a STROKE — one line segment between each consecutive point, tracing the glyph's outline. It does not fill the interior, so it is the verb for line art: an arrow, a chevron, a bracket, the border of a shape. Note the ARGUMENT ORDER carefully — the path comes first, THEN ox, oy, scale, then an optional color (a palette index 0..15 or a color name like 'red). It paints to the framebuffer and returns undefined, so it MUST be preceded by (set-mode 'sd). Because it strokes rather than fills, an open path (no Z) stays open — the last point is NOT joined back to the first — which is exactly what you want for a line that is not a closed loop."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(draw-svg-path \"M12 2 L2 22 L22 22 Z\" 40 40 4 8)" :note "Stroke a triangle glyph at (40,40), sized ×4, in palette color 8 — three edges plus the Z-closing edge back to the top corner.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(draw-svg-path \"M8 4 L16 12 L8 20\" 100 80 3 'cyan)" :note "An OPEN chevron (no Z): the stroke draws two segments and stops — the endpoints are not joined, giving the > shape rather than a triangle.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define bracket \"M14 4 H8 V20 H14\")\n(for-each (lambda (i)\n            (draw-svg-path bracket (+ 30 (* i 50)) 60 (+ 2 i) i))\n          (iota 4))" :note "Stroke a square-bracket glyph (built from H/V axis-lines) four times at increasing scale and color, laying out a row of brackets — line art replicated.")
  )
  :caveats ("Requires (set-mode 'sd) first; without a framebuffer the call is a silent no-op that paints nothing. The examples above all start with set-mode for this reason." "The argument order is (d ox oy scale [color]) — path FIRST, then position, then size. This differs from svg-path->points, whose order is (d scale ox oy steps). Swapping ox/oy with scale is the most common mistake." "The stroke uses the default Bézier flattening (steps = 8) — this verb does not expose a steps argument, so very large curves may look faceted. If you need finer curves, pre-flatten with svg-path->points at higher steps and stroke the points with poly instead.")
  :drawbacks ("Stroke only — it never fills, so a glyph meant to read as a solid shape looks like a wireframe. Reach for fill-svg-path when you want an area." "Line width is a single pixel; there is no thick-stroke or line-cap control. Heavy outlines must be built by overdrawing at small offsets." "Multiple subpaths in one `d` string are drawn as one connected polyline, so the jump between subpaths (e.g. the shaft-to-head of an arrow) draws a connecting segment you may not want; author such glyphs as separate draw calls if that seam shows.")
  :usecases ("Drawing imported line icons — arrows, chevrons, checkmarks, brackets — as outlines." "Overlaying a crisp border on top of a filled shape (stroke after fill) to make an icon pop." "Wireframe / debug rendering of a glyph to see its exact point path before committing to a fill." "Building UI chrome (dividers, frames, cursor marks) from small hand-written path strings.")
  :related ("fill-svg-path" "svg-path->points" "poly" "line")
  :learn (:concept "Stroking a path means drawing a line segment between each consecutive point; open paths stay open, Z-closed paths draw the closing edge." :prerequisites ("svg-path->points" "set-mode" "line") :progression "Stroke a glyph with draw-svg-path; fill one with fill-svg-path; then layer a fill under a stroke to build a two-tone icon.")
  :row-2-audit (

    (:problem "A menu designer wants a back-arrow icon that reads as clean line art, not a solid blob. They have the glyph \"M4 12 H20 M14 6 L20 12 L14 18\" from an icon set and need to stamp it in the header at a fixed size and color, on the real canvas."
     :program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(draw-svg-path \"M4 12 H20 M14 6 L20 12 L14 18\" 20 20 3 7)"
     :eng-program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (icon d x y size palette)\n  (draw-svg-path d x y size palette))\n(icon \"M4 12 H20 M14 6 L20 12 L14 18\" 20 20 3 7)\n(icon \"M4 12 H20 M14 6 L20 12 L14 18\" 20 60 3 9)"
     :math-program "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; Same glyph at two scales: the second must occupy 2× the pixel extent.\n(draw-svg-path \"M4 12 H20 M14 6 L20 12 L14 18\" 20 20 2 7)\n(draw-svg-path \"M4 12 H20 M14 6 L20 12 L14 18\" 20 80 4 7)"
     :cs-program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (safe-stroke d x y size color)\n  (if (and (string? d) (number? x) (number? y) (number? size))\n      (draw-svg-path d x y size color)\n      (error 'bad-icon-args)))\n(safe-stroke \"M4 12 H20 M14 6 L20 12 L14 18\" 20 20 3 7)"
     :explanation "eng: a reusable icon wrapper that names the argument order once so callers stop swapping ox/oy with scale. math: stroking the same glyph at scale 2 and 4 shows the outline extent doubling. cs: a guard that rejects malformed args before painting."
     :meta "fill-svg-path would paint a solid triangle-and-bar, hiding the arrow's line-art character. svg-path->points returns data but paints nothing, so the header stays blank. line could draw the arrow but forces the designer to hand-list every segment endpoint the path already encodes. draw-svg-path is the stroke-a-glyph primitive; picking a sibling either fills when you want outline, or paints nothing, or makes you re-type the geometry.")
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'stroke means outline, not area' — a visual smoke test after an icon-set upgrade is 'does the arrow still look like a line, or did someone swap in the fill verb?'; the seam between subpaths is the thing to watch." :math "The stroke is the union of segments [p_k, p_{k+1}] for consecutive points; an open path omits the [p_n, p_0] closing segment that a Z would add." :cs "A polyline rasterizer: iterate consecutive point pairs, call the framebuffer's line routine on each; cost is O(points) line draws."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Trace a path with a moving pen — connect where you are to where you go next, leaving a visible trail."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "svg-path" :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(draw-svg-path \"M8 4 L16 12 L8 20\" 100 80 3 6)")
       (:domain "plotter"  :code "(define (plot-moves moves)\n  (let loop ((ms moves) (x 0) (y 0) (segments '()))\n    (if (null? ms) (reverse segments)\n        (let ((nx (car (car ms))) (ny (cadr (car ms))))\n          (loop (cdr ms) nx ny (cons (list x y '-> nx ny) segments))))))\n(plot-moves '((8 4) (16 12) (8 20)))")
       (:domain "piecewise-linear" :code "(define (segment-lengths pts)\n  (let loop ((ps pts) (acc '()))\n    (if (or (null? ps) (null? (cdr ps))) (reverse acc)\n        (let* ((a (car ps)) (b (cadr ps))\n               (dx (- (car b) (car a))) (dy (- (cadr b) (cadr a))))\n          (loop (cdr ps) (cons (sqrt (+ (* dx dx) (* dy dy))) acc))))))\n(segment-lengths '((8 4) (16 12) (8 20)))"))
     :explanation "Rung 1: draw-svg-path strokes a glyph by connecting each point to the next with a line. Rung 2: a pen plotter reads move commands and lowers its pen between consecutive positions, leaving ink segments. Rung 3: the outline IS a piecewise-linear function — a chain of straight segments whose lengths sum to the perimeter. All three share the same shape — a sequence of points becomes a connected path by joining neighbors."
     :meta "The transfer works because 'connect consecutive points' is the substrate-independent core of stroking. A CNC toolpath, a pen plotter, an ECG trace, a stock chart — each visits points in order and joins them. The math rung reveals it as a piecewise-linear curve, the simplest non-trivial parametrization of a shape's boundary. Grounds in book-of-math ch-advanced-10 (numerical-methods) as the piecewise-linear-interpolation shape."
     )
     (:eng "Every point-connecting substrate shares the 'unwanted travel move' problem — the jump between two subpaths draws a segment you did not mean to draw; the SRE fix is to lift the pen (separate draw calls) between disjoint strokes." :math "A polyline is the image of a piecewise-linear map [0,1] → ℝ² whose breakpoints are the input points; its length is the sum of segment lengths." :cs "Sequential edge traversal over an ordered vertex list; the shape of every line-chart renderer and toolpath generator."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "Stroking is translation-equivariant: stroking a glyph at (ox, oy) paints the same shape as stroking it at (0,0) then shifting every pixel by (ox, oy) — the offset just moves the whole outline."
     :program "(define d \"M8 4 L16 12 L8 20\")\n(define at0 (svg-path->points d 3 0 0))\n(define at-off (svg-path->points d 3 40 25))\n(display (equal? (map (lambda (p) (list (+ 40 (car p)) (+ 25 (cadr p)))) at0)\n                 at-off))"
     :eng-program "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; The same stroke at two offsets: identical shape, shifted position.\n(draw-svg-path \"M8 4 L16 12 L8 20\" 30 30 3 6)\n(draw-svg-path \"M8 4 L16 12 L8 20\" 130 30 3 6)"
     :math-program "(define (shift-equivariant? d scale ox oy)\n  (let ((a (svg-path->points d scale 0 0))\n        (b (svg-path->points d scale ox oy)))\n    (equal? (map (lambda (p) (list (+ ox (car p)) (+ oy (cadr p)))) a) b)))\n(list (shift-equivariant? \"M8 4 L16 12 L8 20\" 3 40 25)\n      (shift-equivariant? \"M12 2 L2 22 L22 22 Z\" 5 10 60))"
     :cs-program "(define (offset-is-additive? d scale)\n  ;; points at (ox,oy) = points at (0,0) plus (ox,oy), for the whole list.\n  (let* ((base (svg-path->points d scale 0 0))\n         (moved (svg-path->points d scale 17 23)))\n    (equal? moved (map (lambda (p) (list (+ 17 (car p)) (+ 23 (cadr p)))) base))))\n(offset-is-additive? \"M8 4 L16 12 L8 20\" 3)"
     :invariant "∀ d scale ox oy. points(d, scale, ox, oy) = points(d, scale, 0, 0) ⊕ (ox, oy)  (the offset is added uniformly; stroking the glyph anywhere is the same outline, translated)"
     :meta "The CS pattern is translation-equivariance — the transform (where you place the glyph) commutes with the operation (drawing the outline). Falsified if a glyph drawn at an offset were a DIFFERENT shape than the same glyph at the origin — that would mean placement distorts geometry, and no icon could be trusted to look the same at two positions. book-of-math ch-advanced-04 (groups-symmetry) grounds the translation group acting on the plane; book-of-code ch 10 (state-machines) grounds equivariance-under-a-uniform-shift."
     )
    (:eng "The engineering consequence is that a designer can lay out identical icons across a row by varying only ox — the shape is guaranteed identical, so a toolbar of eight arrows is one glyph placed at eight offsets." :math "Translation by (ox,oy) is an isometry of the plane; the point map is that isometry composed with scaling, so shape (up to congruence) is preserved." :cs "Equivariance under a group action; the same shape as 'sort then map f' = 'map f then sort' when f is order-preserving — the operation commutes with the transform."))
  :row-5-emergence (

    (:base "draw-svg-path strokes one glyph outline onto the canvas."
     :composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define star \"M12 2 L15 9 L22 9 L16 14 L18 22 L12 17 L6 22 L8 14 L2 9 L9 9 Z\")\n(for-each (lambda (i)\n            (draw-svg-path star (+ 40 (* i 45)) 60 2 (+ 6 i)))\n          (iota 5))"
     :eng-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; A framed badge: stroke a diamond border, then a plus mark centered in it.\n(draw-svg-path \"M12 2 L22 12 L12 22 L2 12 Z\" 100 60 4 0)\n(draw-svg-path \"M12 6 V18 M6 12 H18\" 100 60 4 7)"
     :math-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; Rotational replication by hand-mirroring a chevron into a 4-pointed mark.\n(draw-svg-path \"M12 4 L16 12 L12 20\" 120 80 3 6)\n(draw-svg-path \"M12 4 L8 12 L12 20\" 120 80 3 6)"
     :cs-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define icons (list \"M4 12 H20 M14 6 L20 12 L14 18\"\n                    \"M8 4 L16 12 L8 20\"\n                    \"M6 12 L10 16 L18 6\"))\n(for-each (lambda (i)\n            (draw-svg-path (list-ref icons (modulo i 3))\n                           (+ 30 (* i 60)) 100 3 (+ 3 i)))\n          (iota 6))"
     :emergent "A toolbar: a strip of stroked icons — arrows, chevrons, checkmarks — laid out at even offsets, or a badge built by nesting a bordered frame around a centered mark. Together they are a line-art icon composer, structurally the same as an SVG sprite sheet drawn to a raster surface."
     :meta "This is the vector-graphics-import / line-art-composition family — a glyph library rendered as strokes and arranged by transform. Familiar analogs are an icon font laid out in a menu bar, a sprite atlas blitted at grid positions, and a vector UI toolkit stroking widget outlines. The composition works because draw-svg-path is translation-equivariant (Row 4), so each icon in the strip is the same trusted outline shifted by its slot. Grounds in book-of-code ch 14 (assembly-then-higher) as the render-a-library-of-glyphs shape."
     )
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'nested strokes must not fight' — when you frame a mark inside a border, both strokes share the framebuffer, so draw the border first and the mark second; the SRE probe is 'does the inner mark stay legible against the outer frame?'" :math "The arrangement is the union of translated copies of glyph outlines; a badge is the union of a boundary curve and an interior curve sharing a center." :cs "A display list of stroke commands rendered in order; the shape of every retained-mode 2D scene graph and every SVG <g> group flattened to a raster."))
)

(verb
  :name "fill-svg-path" :std "Motoi"
  :library "svg-path"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(fill-svg-path d ox oy scale [color]) -> undefined"
  :summary "Fill the interior of an SVG path as a solid area using the even-odd rule; requires (set-mode 'sd)."
  :explanation "This is the area-painting partner to draw-svg-path. It parses the `d` string, places it at (ox, oy) at `scale`, and then SCANLINE-FILLS the enclosed region: for each horizontal row of pixels it finds where the polygon's edges cross that row and paints the spans between crossings, using the EVEN-ODD rule (a pixel is inside when a ray from it crosses the boundary an odd number of times). The result is a solid glyph — a filled heart, a solid star, a colored badge — not just its outline. The argument order matches draw-svg-path exactly: (d ox oy scale [color]). Like every painting verb it needs (set-mode 'sd) and returns undefined. Even-odd is what makes ring-shaped glyphs (an O, a donut, a frame) render with a hole in the middle when the path winds the outer and inner loops in opposite directions."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(fill-svg-path \"M12 2 L2 22 L22 22 Z\" 40 40 4 11)" :note "Fill a triangle glyph solid in palette color 11 — the whole interior is painted, not just the edges.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(fill-svg-path \"M10 4 H14 V10 H20 V14 H14 V20 H10 V14 H4 V10 H10 Z\" 100 60 6 'green)" :note "Fill a plus-sign glyph built entirely from H/V axis-lines — a cross shape solid-filled by scanline, sized ×6.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define diamond \"M12 2 L22 12 L12 22 L2 12 Z\")\n(for-each\n  (lambda (r)\n    (for-each\n      (lambda (c)\n        (fill-svg-path diamond (+ 30 (* c 40)) (+ 30 (* r 40)) 2\n                       (+ 1 (modulo (+ r c) 14))))\n      (iota 5)))\n  (iota 3))" :note "Tile a 3×5 grid of filled diamonds, cycling the palette index by (row+col) — a solid-glyph mosaic built from one imported shape.")
  )
  :caveats ("Even-odd fill, not nonzero-winding: for a SELF-INTERSECTING path (a pentagram drawn as one crossing loop) the crossings cancel, so the center comes out UNFILLED. If you need the solid star, author the outline as a non-crossing 10-point polygon instead of a 5-point crossing one." "Requires (set-mode 'sd); with no framebuffer it paints nothing and returns undefined silently." "Curve commands flatten at the default steps = 8 (no steps argument is exposed here), so a filled shape with large curves has a slightly polygonal edge. Higher-fidelity fills need pre-flattening with svg-path->points and fill-poly." "Multiple subpaths are merged into one polygon for the even-odd test — a glyph whose inner and outer loops wind the SAME direction will fill the hole solid instead of leaving it open.")
  :drawbacks ("No anti-aliasing: edges are hard pixel steps, so a filled glyph at small scale can look jagged, especially on diagonals." "The fill is flat color — no gradients, patterns, or texture. Reach for repeated fills at offsets if you want banding." "Cost is O(height × edges) because every scanline tests every edge; a high-scale glyph with many points over a tall bounding box is the expensive case.")
  :usecases ("Rendering a solid imported glyph — a filled heart, a solid arrow, a badge — as a colored area." "Building the base layer of a two-tone icon (fill first, stroke on top with draw-svg-path)." "Tiling or stamping a solid shape across a grid to make a pattern or mosaic." "Coloring UI elements (buttons, chips, status dots) from small hand-written path strings.")
  :related ("draw-svg-path" "svg-path->points" "fill-poly" "poly")
  :learn (:concept "Filling a path means scanline-testing each row against the polygon edges and painting the inside spans, here by the even-odd rule." :prerequisites ("svg-path->points" "set-mode" "fill-poly") :progression "Fill a glyph solid with fill-svg-path; stroke its border with draw-svg-path; then layer fill-then-stroke to build a finished two-tone icon.")
  :row-2-audit (

    (:problem "A status widget needs a solid colored dot-diamond as a health indicator — green when good, red when bad. The designer has a diamond glyph and wants it painted as a filled area whose color they can switch, on the real canvas."
     :program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define diamond \"M12 2 L22 12 L12 22 L2 12 Z\")\n(fill-svg-path diamond 40 40 3 'green)"
     :eng-program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define diamond \"M12 2 L22 12 L12 22 L2 12 Z\")\n(define (status-dot x y healthy?)\n  (fill-svg-path diamond x y 3 (if healthy? 'green 'red)))\n(status-dot 40 40 #t)\n(status-dot 100 40 #f)"
     :math-program "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; A filled shape at scale 4 covers 4× the linear extent of the same at scale 1;\n;; paint both to see the area grow with the square of scale.\n(define diamond \"M12 2 L22 12 L12 22 L2 12 Z\")\n(fill-svg-path diamond 40 40 1 11)\n(fill-svg-path diamond 120 40 4 11)"
     :cs-program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define diamond \"M12 2 L22 12 L12 22 L2 12 Z\")\n(define (fill-or-skip d x y size color)\n  (if (>= size 1) (fill-svg-path d x y size color) 'too-small))\n(fill-or-skip diamond 40 40 3 'green)"
     :explanation "eng: a status-dot wrapper that maps a health boolean to a fill color. math: filling the same glyph at scale 1 and 4 shows the painted AREA grow as scale². cs: a guard that skips degenerate sub-pixel fills."
     :meta "draw-svg-path would stroke only the diamond's border, leaving the middle transparent — a hollow indicator that reads poorly at a glance. svg-path->points returns the corner list but paints nothing. fill-poly could fill it but needs the point list built first, duplicating the parse. fill-svg-path is the fill-a-glyph-string primitive; picking a sibling gives an outline, or no paint, or a redundant parse step.")
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'fill means solid area' — a designer swapping the stroke verb for the fill verb by mistake yields a hollow dot; the smoke test is 'is the center painted?'. The color argument accepts a name or an index, so a health map from boolean to symbol works." :math "The filled region is the set of pixels with odd crossing number against the polygon boundary; its area grows as scale² while the outline length grows as scale." :cs "A scanline polygon-fill: per row, collect edge-crossing x-values, sort them, paint span pairs; cost O(height × edges)."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Decide inside-versus-outside for a region, then color everything inside — a membership test swept across a grid."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "svg-path" :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(fill-svg-path \"M12 2 L22 12 L12 22 L2 12 Z\" 60 60 4 11)")
       (:domain "flood-region" :code "(define (inside-diamond? cx cy r x y)\n  (<= (+ (abs (- x cx)) (abs (- y cy))) r))\n(map (lambda (x) (list x (inside-diamond? 5 5 3 x 5)))\n     (iota 11))")
       (:domain "even-odd-crossing" :code "(define (crossings-odd? edges-crossed) (odd? edges-crossed))\n(map crossings-odd? '(0 1 2 3 4))"))
     :explanation "Rung 1: fill-svg-path decides which pixels are inside the glyph and paints them. Rung 2: a diamond membership predicate answers inside/outside for any (x,y) via an L1-distance test. Rung 3: the even-odd rule itself is just 'is the number of boundary crossings odd?' All three share the same shape — an inside/outside test is evaluated over a region, and the answer selects what gets colored."
     :meta "The transfer works because 'fill a region' is 'evaluate a membership predicate everywhere and paint the trues'. A GPU fragment shader, a flood-fill, a set-membership sweep, a level-set — each colors a region by a point-inclusion test. The even-odd rung exposes the specific predicate this verb uses. Grounds in book-of-math ch-advanced-15 (dynamical-systems-chaos-fractals) as the region-membership / basin shape."
     )
     (:eng "Every region-fill substrate faces the same edge case — points exactly ON the boundary, and self-intersections where the inside/outside test flips unexpectedly; the SRE lens is 'test a known-hollow glyph and a known-solid one, confirm both look right'." :math "The even-odd rule is the parity of the winding-crossing count; a point is inside iff a generic ray from it crosses the boundary an odd number of times." :cs "Point-in-polygon by ray casting, batched per scanline so each row is one sorted sweep instead of a per-pixel test; cost O(height × edges)."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "Fill and stroke share the SAME geometry: the point list fill-svg-path colors inside is exactly the point list draw-svg-path traces around — filling and stroking a glyph agree on its outline."
     :program "(define d \"M12 2 L22 12 L12 22 L2 12 Z\")\n;; Both painters call the same parser with the same (ox oy scale) order,\n;; so both operate on this identical point list:\n(define pts (svg-path->points d 3 40 40))\n(display (= (length pts) 5))\n(newline)\n;; closed: first point equals last (the Z edge both share)\n(display (equal? (car pts) (car (reverse pts))))"
     :eng-program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define d \"M12 2 L22 12 L12 22 L2 12 Z\")\n;; Layer them: fill the area, then stroke the SAME outline on top.\n(fill-svg-path d 100 60 5 3)\n(draw-svg-path d 100 60 5 0)"
     :math-program "(define (fill-stroke-same-boundary? d scale ox oy)\n  ;; The boundary both painters use is the polygon through these points.\n  (let ((pts (svg-path->points d scale ox oy)))\n    (and (>= (length pts) 3)\n         (equal? (car pts) (car (reverse pts))))))\n(list (fill-stroke-same-boundary? \"M12 2 L22 12 L12 22 L2 12 Z\" 4 0 0)\n      (fill-stroke-same-boundary? \"M12 2 L2 22 L22 22 Z\" 3 10 10))"
     :cs-program "(define (parse-order-consistent? d)\n  ;; fill-svg-path and draw-svg-path both take (d ox oy scale): a glyph\n  ;; placed at the same args produces one point set for both.\n  (let ((a (svg-path->points d 2 30 30))\n        (b (svg-path->points d 2 30 30)))\n    (equal? a b)))\n(parse-order-consistent? \"M12 2 L22 12 L12 22 L2 12 Z\")"
     :invariant "∀ d ox oy scale. boundary(fill-svg-path(d,ox,oy,scale)) = boundary(draw-svg-path(d,ox,oy,scale)) = points(d,scale,ox,oy)  (fill and stroke agree on the outline; a closed path returns to its start)"
     :meta "The CS pattern is that two operations over one shared representation must AGREE on that representation — fill and stroke are two interpretations of the same parsed point list, so a filled glyph and a stroked glyph line up pixel-for-pixel at the boundary. Falsified if a filled glyph's edge did not coincide with the stroked outline of the same path — that would mean the two painters parse the string differently, and you could never cleanly stroke a border onto a fill. book-of-math ch-advanced-05 grounds shared-object identity; book-of-code ch 05 grounds two-consumers-one-value consistency."
     )
    (:eng "The engineering consequence is that a two-tone icon — fill under, stroke over — registers perfectly, because both painters place the SAME point list at the SAME coordinates; the operator never has to nudge one to match the other." :math "Fill computes the region bounded by the polygon; stroke computes that polygon's boundary; the two are the interior and the frontier of the same point set, which share the same edge." :cs "Two consumers of one shared value; the same shape as a JSON object read by two parsers that must agree, or an AST both a type-checker and a code-gen walk."))
  :row-5-emergence (

    (:base "fill-svg-path paints one glyph solid onto the canvas."
     :composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; A house it has never seen: a filled roof triangle over a filled wall square.\n(fill-svg-path \"M4 12 L12 4 L20 12 Z\" 80 60 4 4)\n(fill-svg-path \"M6 12 H18 V22 H6 Z\" 80 60 4 6)"
     :eng-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; A finished badge: fill the diamond body, then stroke its border for a rim.\n(define d \"M12 2 L22 12 L12 22 L2 12 Z\")\n(fill-svg-path d 120 70 5 9)\n(draw-svg-path d 120 70 5 0)"
     :math-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; Concentric fills: the same glyph at shrinking scale in alternating color\n;; paints a bullseye — nested regions from one shape.\n(define d \"M12 2 L22 12 L12 22 L2 12 Z\")\n(for-each (lambda (s) (fill-svg-path d 160 90 s (if (even? s) 11 3)))\n          (list 6 4 2))"
     :cs-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; A palette strip: one arrow glyph filled across a row in every color.\n(define arrow \"M4 12 L14 12 L14 6 L22 14 L14 22 L14 16 L4 16 Z\")\n(for-each (lambda (i)\n            (fill-svg-path arrow (+ 20 (* i 24)) 110 1 (+ 1 i)))\n          (iota 12))"
     :emergent "A composite object it was never taught: a HOUSE assembled from a filled roof triangle and a filled wall square, a two-tone BADGE from fill-then-stroke, or a BULLSEYE from concentric fills of one glyph. Together they are compositional-geometry from imported shapes — solid regions layered by z-order — structurally the same as constructive solid geometry or a painter's-algorithm scene."
     :meta "This is the compositional-geometry / retained-fill family — solid glyphs stacked in draw order to build recognizable objects the corpus never enumerated. Familiar analogs are a painter's-algorithm renderer (back-to-front fills), CSG union of solids, and a layered image editor. The composition works because each fill shares its boundary with its stroke (Row 4) and every placement is faithful (Row 2's linearity), so a roof lands exactly on its wall and a rim lands exactly on its body. Grounds in book-of-code ch 14 (assembly-then-higher) as the layer-primitives-into-an-object shape."
     )
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'z-order is the API' — fills paint over what came before, so the operator must order calls back-to-front (wall then roof would hide the wall's top edge); the SRE probe is 'does the last fill occlude something it should not?'" :math "Layering fills is the painter's algorithm: the final color of a pixel is the topmost region containing it; a composite object is a union of solid regions ordered by depth." :cs "Back-to-front compositing / display list of filled polygons; the shape of every 2D scene renderer and every CSG-union of areas."))
)
